1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Section 2 of General Aptitude asks for five computations, and every question in it is one of them.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
A ratio question asks for a share of one total. A data question chains a percentage to a unit.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
A logarithm question turns a product into a sum. A mensuration question turns a length into a volume.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
A statistics question names a centre. A probability question counts the complement.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Coins of one, five and ten rupees sit in the ratio five to three to thirteen.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Multiply each share by its denomination before you compare anything.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The values are five, fifteen and a hundred and thirty: one hundred and fifty rupees in total.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Fifteen out of a hundred and fifty is ten per cent. The official key marks option C.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The chart gives shares of two thousand kilocalories; the table gives kilocalories per gram.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Multiply the share by the total, then divide by the rate. Two steps, and the units cancel.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The three fats take forty-five per cent of two thousand: nine hundred kilocalories.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
All three fats are nine kilocalories per gram, so nine hundred divided by nine is one hundred grams.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
The logarithm of a product is the sum of the logarithms, and the logarithm of a power is the exponent times the logarithm.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The condition is log of p squared plus q squared, equals log p plus log q plus two log three.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Exponentiate both sides: p squared plus q squared equals nine times p q.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Then p to the fourth plus q to the fourth is eighty-one minus two, so seventy-nine times p squared q squared. The answer is seventy-nine.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The sheet is fifty-four by four, so its area is two hundred and sixteen square centimetres.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Joining the two longer edges makes the tube fifty-four tall with a circumference of four.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The cube has the same surface area, so a side is six and its volume is two hundred and sixteen.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The tube is two hundred and sixteen over pi, so the ratio is one over pi.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Ten numbers. Sort them first: the median is a position in the sorted list, not a value you can see in the original order.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
With ten values there is no middle one, so the median is the average of the fifth and sixth: thirteen point five.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The mean is eighteen point seven, because sixty-nine pulls it up. The median did not move.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Five of the ten values lie above the median: exactly half, and never more.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
At least one head in three tosses. Counting the cases directly means listing eight outcomes.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The complement is one case, three tails, so the answer is one minus one eighth.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Seven eighths. The complement turned eight cases into one.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
In a bag of five red and three blue, two drawn without replacement: twenty-five over twenty-eight have at least one red.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Each term is the sum of the two before it. Given the sixth and seventh, run the recurrence backwards.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Subtract: the fifth term is sixty minus thirty-seven, twenty-three.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Then fourteen, then nine, then five, then four. The first term is four.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Forward again: four, five, nine, fourteen, twenty-three, thirty-seven, sixty. That is the check.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Two to the tenth is one thousand and twenty-four, so ten doublings are about three decimal digits.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
That is why log base ten of one thousand is about three: a factor of a thousand is ten doublings.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Five per cent growth doubles in about fourteen years: one point zero five to the fourteenth is one point nine eight.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The rule of seventy-two says seventy-two divided by five, fourteen point four. Estimation names the size; exact work names the digits.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Step one: name the base, and write the total it is a base of.

38
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Step two: write the chain with its units, and cancel them before you compute.

39
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Step three: for a shape, find the quantity that is conserved, and the one power that connects the rest.

40
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Step four: sort before you take a median, and count before you take a probability.

41
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Step five: estimate first, compute second, and check the answer against the estimate.

